INT. HOLLOWAY CIDER MILL - PRESS ROOM - NIGHT

A long timber barn that smells of fermenting apples and wet wood. Wooden presses with iron screws, barrels stacked to the rafters. A lantern on a hook sways in a draft nobody can find. Bees drowse in the beams, drunk on sugar, their hum a low drone.

JUNIPER HOLLOWAY, 50s, mill owner, apron stained russet to the elbows, ladles pulp into a cloth. Her braid is thick as a rope.

RHYS DOYLE, 30s, hired hand, leans on a broom and doesn't sweep. BEATA NOWAK, 60s, Juniper's sister, sits on an upturned crate peeling an apple in one unbroken ribbon. COLM, 20s, the grandson, perches on a barrel with a harmonica he isn't playing. OLUWASEUN ADEYEMI, 40s, the buyer from the city, in a camel coat that doesn't belong here, taps a clipboard.

JUNIPER
(to the room)
Last press of the season. Then we can all go home.

RHYS
Or stay.

BEATA
No one's staying.

OLUWASEUN
I do need a count by midnight.

JUNIPER
You'll have it.

She turns the screw. Juice runs into a trough, amber and slow. The sound is oddly soft.

COLM
It sounds like someone breathing.

BEATA
It's the cider, Colm.

COLM
Not the cider. Under it.

They go still. The juice drips. The bees hum. And beneath it all, a slow, wet inhale. Then an exhale.

RHYS
(muttering)
Pipes.

JUNIPER
There are no pipes in the press room.

OLUWASEUN
Is there a basement?

Juniper and Beata exchange a look so quick it's nearly nothing.

BEATA
No basement.

JUNIPER
We don't dig here.

OLUWASEUN
Why not?

JUNIPER
The ground doesn't like it.

Oluwaseun smiles, thinks it a joke. Nobody smiles back.

The lantern swings. Its light crosses Rhys's face, Beata's hands, Colm's eyes, Juniper's braid. The hum of bees thickens.

Juniper lifts a ladle to her lips. Tastes. Her face doesn't change.

JUNIPER (CONT'D)
(to Colm)
Put your harmonica down, love.

COLM
I wasn't--

JUNIPER
Now.

He sets it on the barrel. It rolls an inch. Stops.

Beata stops peeling. The ribbon of apple skin hangs, quivering, in her fingers.

BEATA
(softly)
It's early.

JUNIPER
It's early.

OLUWASEUN
What is?

The breathing quickens. The barrel under Colm shudders. The staves bulge, as if something inside is leaning out.

All five freeze. The bees fall silent in the rafters.

Then, in one motion, Rhys drops the broom, Beata drops the apple, Colm scrambles off the barrel, Oluwaseun stumbles backward over the trough, and Juniper turns calmly, takes a mallet from a hook, and faces the barrel as if she'd been expecting it since March.

JUNIPER (CONT'D)
(to all)
Out. Quiet. Don't run.

They go. Slowly. Colm last, looking back.

Juniper steps to the barrel. Raises the mallet. Waits.

The staves creak. The breath hitches. Waits with her.
